# Reviewed self-development release checklist

Use this checklist before a Formal AI release is versioned or tagged. Each
item protects the reviewed, session-backed contribution loop required by E77.

## 1. Refresh the release boundary

- Fetch `origin/main` before measuring the candidate branch.
- Fetch annotated tags so the latest release boundary exists locally.
- Confirm the newest ledger row names the newest fetched release tag.
- Resolve the tag to a commit instead of trusting a local branch name.
- Measure the exact `<latest-tag>..HEAD` range used by release automation.
- Stop if the checkout cannot prove which tag begins the release cycle.

## 2. Identify the authored contribution

- Require at least one non-merge commit with a Formal AI session trailer.
- Require the matching evidence trailer in the same commit message.
- Require a canonical GitHub pull-request URL in the PR trailer.
- Reject partial trailer sets instead of guessing the missing identity.
- Keep captured logs separate from the changed-line authorship calculation.
- Verify the committed evidence names both Formal AI and the session id.

## 3. Prove ordinary pull-request review

- Walk first-parent merge history for the release range.
- Match the pull-request number in the merge subject to the trailer URL.
- Inspect the merge commit's second parent as the reviewed branch tip.
- Require the attributed commit object on that second-parent branch.
- Require the same object to be absent from the merge's first parent.
- Reject a direct commit that merely claims a pull-request URL.

## 4. Project the non-decreasing target

- Read only ledger rows produced by the current metric definition.
- Use the configured trailing release window without mixing epochs.
- Project the candidate release without writing a ledger row.
- Compare the candidate projection with the durable release target.
- Compare PR-time impact with the no-op base projection.
- Add real session-backed work when the candidate would lower the share.

## 5. Keep retries idempotent

- Exclude an existing row for the tag from its own retry projection.
- Accept a repeated release record only when every field is identical.
- Reject duplicate tag rows that disagree with committed history.
- Leave version files unchanged until eligibility succeeds.
- Re-run the same precondition after fetching newer tags or main commits.
- Preserve the open release range when more reviewed work is required.

## 6. Preserve existing trust gates

- Do not grant Formal AI merge, approval, or promotion authority.
- Send authored bytes through ordinary human review.
- Require the same CI matrix used for other repository changes.
- Keep trusted promotion rules in force for learned runtime behavior.
- Treat the release gate as evidence verification, not authorization.
- Record failures without bypassing branch protection.

## 7. Validate before version mutation

- Run the self-development release gate before editing Cargo metadata.
- Confirm a qualifying reviewed PR exists in the current release range.
- Confirm the projected share meets the non-decreasing target.
- Confirm the working tree is clean before automated version changes.
- Abort without partial edits when any precondition fails.
- Re-run after remediation instead of increasing a timeout or threshold.

## 8. Record the successful release

- Store the release tag, range endpoints, and canonical until commit.
- Store authored and total changed-line counts.
- Store authored and total non-merge commit counts.
- Store the per-release and trailing percentages.
- Store the target that governed the release.
- Store every qualifying Formal AI-authored pull-request URL.

## 9. Publish reviewable evidence

- Include the self-hosting share in generated release notes.
- Include the trailing share and non-decreasing target.
- Link the reviewed pull requests that satisfied the release floor.
- Preserve raw Agent CLI output for replay and audit.
- Preserve the Formal AI server trace for the recorded session.
- Keep the exact authored artifact beside its evidence bundle.

## 10. Investigate a red ratchet

- Verify the run used the newest commit and release tag.
- Compare timestamps and head SHA before diagnosing stale CI data.
- Preserve the complete failed workflow log locally.
- Find the exact numerator and denominator behind both projections.
- Attribute only work actually produced by a recorded Formal AI session.
- Split unrelated work instead of weakening the target.

## 11. Finalize the pull request

- Merge the latest default branch and resolve generated-file drift.
- Run focused regressions before the full local CI-equivalent suite.
- Verify strict lint, examples, policies, and derived artifacts.
- Push only the prepared issue branch.
- Confirm every required check belongs to the pushed head SHA.
- Mark the pull request ready only after all required checks pass.

Completion means the release contains real Formal AI work whose unchanged
bytes passed normal review, CI, promotion rules, and the self-hosting ratchet.
